    "title": "Why does my 14 month old baby wakes up crying asking for water and banana in the night",
    "body": "<p>I exclusively breastfed my baby and he sleeps with us. Needs my touch. Wakes up 3-4 times. 3 times for water once for banana now it is a habit.</p>\n"
  },
  "answers": [
    {
      "owner": {
        "account_id": 9754817,
        "reputation": 2492,
        "user_id": 42067,
        "user_type": "registered",
        "profile_image": "https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e8983c003003ba97dc6398b3be5b5bb3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2",
        "display_name": "R Davies",
        "link": "https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/42067/r-davies"
      },
      "is_accepted": false,
      "score": 4,
      "last_activity_date": 1718007032,
      "creation_date": 1718007032,
      "answer_id": 44198,
      "question_id": 44197,
      "content_license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "body": "<p>Make sure your child is drinking plenty in the day, offering milk (breast or otherwise) and water often and refilling their cup/bottle often.</p>\n<p>Also make sure that your child's pre-bedtime meal is going to keep them going all night, including protein (animal or plant based), fat and fibre. Maybe swap to brown rice/bread or add a nice thick yoghurt for dessert.</p>\n<p>If they do wake up thirsty, and well, we all do sometimes if it's hot or we have a cold for example. A few sips of water and then cuddle back to sleep. I'd keep a suitable sippy cup of water close and keep any wakeups quiet and calm.</p>\n<p>I often found we'd get a bad patch of sleep which would last a week or two, maybe coinciding with a growth spurt or a new development in my child, and he'd go back to sleeping better once he was done developing.</p>\n"
    }
  ],
